<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaktism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shakta, Shaktas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shaktism is a major tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> that worships the goddess, Devi or Shakti, as the supreme power of the universe. Based on texts such as the Devi Mahatmya and the Tantras, it reveres goddesses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>, Kali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakhya-temple">Kamakhya</a>. It has been especially strong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, where many medieval kings were patrons of goddess temples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shaktism.jpg" alt="Shaktism" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nomu420 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statues_of_Vaishnavi,_Varahi,_Indrani_and_Camunda,_National_Museum,_New_Delhi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest archaeological evidence of what appears to be an Upper Paleolithic shrine for Shakti worship comes from the site of Baghor I in the Sidhi district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, dated to between 9000 and 8000 BCE. The origins of Shakti worship may also be traced to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>. Among the earliest evidence of reverence for the female aspect of the deity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> is the Devi Suktam hymn, chapter 10.125 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rig Veda</a>. By the mid first millennium CE, goddesses such as Parvati, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>, Kali, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati">Saraswati</a> were already prominent. In eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, after the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, Hindu and Buddhist goddesses were combined to form the Mahavidya, a group of ten goddesses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Shaktas regard Shakti as the supreme, eternal reality of all existence, the same as the concept of Brahman in wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, and as the source, embodiment and energy of the cosmos. Shaktism does not reject the masculine but rejects dualisms such as male and female, soul and body, affirming nature as divine. The Devi Mahatmya is considered as important as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, and other revered scriptures include the Devi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana">Bhagavata Purana</a>, the Kalika Purana and the Shakta Upanishads. A Shakta worships a personally chosen goddess, the ishta-devi, selected by family tradition, region, guru lineage or personal resonance.</p><p>Its two largest schools are the Srikula, the family of Tripura Sundari, which is strongest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a> and has been a force there since no later than the seventh century, and the Kalikula, the family of Kali, which prevails in northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Srividya, the best known school of the Srikula, views the goddess as benign and beautiful, while the Kalikula focuses on fierce forms such as Kali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The most important Shakta festival is Navaratri, the nine nights, followed by the tenth day, Dusshera or Vijayadashami, which celebrates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>'s victory over demons described in the Devi Mahatmya. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> the last four days are called Durga Puja and mark her slaying of the buffalo demon Mahishasura. Animal sacrifice to goddesses such as Kali is practised in parts of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the Himalayan states and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, sometimes with a vegetable or sweet substitute, and many devotees find it distasteful.</p><p>Shakta temples are found across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, and the major pilgrimage sites are called Shakta pithas, seats of the Devi. There has been significant sharing of ideas between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana">Tantric Buddhism</a> and Shakta Tantra. The Buddhist Aurangabad Caves of the 6th to 7th century show mother goddesses next to the Buddha, and the Sikh Dasam Granth includes sections on Chandi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shaktism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal patronage of <b>Shaktism</b> alienated many Vaishnavite satras, and the persecution of the Moamaria monastery and its largely lower-caste and tribal followers set off the Moamoria rebellion in 1769.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Hinduism, in the forms of Vaishnavism, Kaumaram, <b>Shaktism</b>, Shaivism and Dravidian folk religion, together with Jainism and Buddhism, was present in the Tamil country from at least the 2nd century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chutias were a Bodo-Kachari people whose rulers adopted Hindu forms of kingship, patronising <b>Shakta</b> worship, notably the Tamreswari temple near Sadiya.</blockquote><a href="/state/chutia-kingdom">Chutia kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings made land grants to Brahmins recorded on copper plates; the kingdom traded silk, aloe wood and elephants, and the goddess shrine at Kamakhya became a major centre of <b>Shakta</b> worship.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamarupa">Kamarupa</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Shakta</b> Hinduism and local cults dominated.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamata-kingdom">Kamata Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
